notes.body:
[BLOCKING] This `in` guard is a stale-codegen workaround that should come out before merge, and while it does not change behavior today, it disables the check that would catch it if the wiring ever broke.

`temperatureFactors` is now selected in `HighlightPanelFields`, which is spread into `PanelFields` then `ReportPanelFields`, and `AnnotatedPanel` is derived from `PanelFieldsFragment`. So after `bun run codegen` (which CI runs on every pipeline, since `generated/graphql.ts` is not committed) `AnnotatedPanel` really does carry `temperatureFactors`, and the comment's premise no longer holds.

The reason to fix it rather than leave it: `'temperatureFactors' in panel` turns a *compile-time* guarantee into a *runtime* one. Today the type system fails the build if that field is dropped from the fragment; with the `in` check in place the same regression compiles clean and silently returns `[]`, so half of this highlight just stops rendering, with no test failure (the unit fixtures hand-build the leaves) and no type error.

Dropping just the guard:

```ts
function panelTemperatureFactors(panel: AnnotatedPanel): TemperatureLeaves {
  const factors = primaryReporter(panel)?.temperatureFactors;
  return Array.isArray(factors) ? factors : [];
}
```

If that does not type-check locally, it means codegen has not been re-run since `highlights.graphql` changed. Running it is the fix, not the guard.

_(Edited: my first version also called the `Array.isArray` normalization redundant because `filterByValue` re-checks. Withdrawing that: `temperatureFactors` really is a bare leaf rather than an array when the panel has no factors, so normalizing here is a contract this helper is entitled to offer.)_

The new test covers the happy path and the empty path, but not the one that actually regressed last quarter: a panel whose factors arrive **out of order**. The renderer sorts by `recordedAt`, and the fixture here is already sorted, so the sort could be deleted and this suite would stay green.

A third case with two factors in reverse order, asserting the rendered order, would pin it:

```ts
test("renders factors oldest first regardless of payload order", () => {
  const panel = panelWith([factor("2025-02-01"), factor("2025-01-01")]);
  expect(renderedDates(panel)).toEqual(["2025-01-01", "2025-02-01"]);
});
```

That is the only thing I would want before this lands.
